{
  "dom_id": "step-q121720",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=\u27e8-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\u27e9,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\u27e8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
